{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.SearchCoercivity","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SearchCoercivity","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.BigOperators.Group.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SearchCoercivity.le_of_le_join","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SearchCoercivity.le_of_le_join","kind":"theorem","line":124,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SearchCoercivity.lean#L124-L132","doc":"**The finite window.** Contrapositive of `join_lt_of_lt_sum`: a pair that matches the\nincumbent `V` lies in `a + b ≤ L`, so the maximisation is over a finite set. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SearchCoercivity.join_lt_of_lt_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SearchCoercivity.join_lt_of_lt_sum","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SearchCoercivity.lean#L109-L122","doc":"**The reduction.** With `f` and `g` bounded above by `B` and `h` below `V - 2B` past `L`,\na pair whose sum exceeds `L` cannot reach `V`.\n\nNothing here knows what `f`, `g` or `h` are: it is the observation that a bounded-plus-bounded\nsum cannot rescue a term that has fallen far enough. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SearchCoercivity.sub_le_neg_mul_of_antitone_step","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SearchCoercivity.sub_le_neg_mul_of_antitone_step","kind":"theorem","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SearchCoercivity.lean#L87-L107","doc":"**Linearisation, downward.** The same inequality on the other side of `a`: climbing `D`\nplaces *gains* at least `D` copies of the first step, so the signed statement is uniform.\n\nThis is the half an agent drops, and dropping it is what makes the bound in the `REV_n` arc\ndiverge — the negative pair terms are exactly what stops a long alternating displacement from\ncollecting the largest step over and over at linear cost. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SearchCoercivity.sub_le_mul_of_antitone_step","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SearchCoercivity.sub_le_mul_of_antitone_step","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SearchCoercivity.lean#L68-L85","doc":"**Linearisation, upward.** If the downward steps `δ` of `e` are antitone, then dropping `D`\nplaces costs at most `D` copies of the *first* step.\n\n`δ` antitone is the discrete form of \"`e` is convex decreasing\": later steps are no larger than\nearlier ones, so the first one dominates the average. ","signatureChunk":0}]}